## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillatory Motion and Waves)
## DBsection(Uniform Circular Motion and Simple Harmonic Motion)
## Date(December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Brandon Lostracco)
## Edited (Connor Wilson, May 1, 2018)
## TitleText1('College Physics')
## AuthorText1('Urone et. al')
## EditionText1('2017')
## Section1('16.6')
## Problem1('1')
## KEYWORDS(frequency, period, harmonic, Hooke's, energy, potential, spring)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$mass   = random(75,      100);
$force  = random(1400000, 1600000, 1000);
$length = random(0.001,   0.009,   0.0001);

$A1 = sqrt(($force) / ($mass)) * ($length);
$A2 = (0.5) * ($force) * ($length)**(2);

BEGIN_PGML
a) What is the maximum velocity of a [`[$mass]`]-[`\textrm{kg}`] person bouncing on a bathroom scale having a stiffness constant of [`[$force]`] [`\textrm{N/m}`], if the amplitude of the bounce is [`[$length]`] [`\textrm{m}`]?

    [_]{$A1}  [`\textrm{m/s}`]

b) What is the maximum energy stored in the spring?

    [_]{$A2}  [`\textrm{J}`]

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Recall the formula for the total mechanical energy stored within a simple harmonic oscillator.

a)  At what displacement from equilibrium is the object's kinetic energy (and thus its velocity) maximized?

b) At what displacement from equilibrium is the object's elastic potential energy maximized?
END_PGML_HINT

ENDDOCUMENT();
